You click an old link to the minutes from January and find the
February minutes instead. The document editor synchronized the latest
change to every device, including the one you had been counting on
as a spare. You need to go back to a particular version, not find one
more copy of the current file. A useful backup lets you name what you
want back, find it, and restore it somewhere safe.

\begin{objectives}
\item Tell a synchronized copy from a recoverable historical copy.
\item Bundle a small document folder and preserve two distinct versions.
\item Restore and verify one version without replacing any working files.
\end{objectives}

\section{Start with a loss you want to withstand}\label{sec:41-plan}
\index{backup}\index{retention}\index{restore}
If you lost a laptop on a trip, you would want a copy away from the
laptop. If you overwrote a document at home, a copy elsewhere of the
\emph{overwritten} document wouldn't help. If a provider closed
your account, a copy under that same account might be inaccessible.
Each of these is a different loss, and no slogan settles all three
questions. Write down the documents to protect, how far back you
might need to go, how much recent work you can afford to redo, and
how long you can reasonably spend getting back to work. A volunteer's
monthly newsletter and the current client invoices probably deserve
different recovery schedules.

Synchronization attempts to bring locations into agreement.
Replication can protect against a single device failing, but agreement
can also propagate an unwanted change. Some sync products offer real
version history; check how long it lasts and whether deletion or
account loss takes that history away. A backup retains a separate
point in time according to a schedule and policy you can test.
A recovery point is the version you can go back to. If a daily
backup ran at breakfast, it generally can't recover the edits you
made at noon before the crash. Keeping multiple dates protects you
when you notice a bad change only later.
In Figure~\ref{fig:41-history}, only the second row preserves a choice.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-41/history.tikz}
\caption{Sync can spread an overwrite; retained versions preserve choices.}\label{fig:41-history}
\VisualDescription{One row shows the newest version replacing an older synchronized copy. A second row keeps both versions as separate recovery points.}
\end{figure}

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.45in}Y}
\toprule
Problem to survive & What to look for in the plan \\
\midrule
Accidental edit or deletion & Distinct versions older than the mistake and a rule for keeping them long enough to notice. \\
Failed or lost device & A copy not on that device or its internal disk. A folder on the same disk is not enough. \\
Lost account or key & A separately accessible recovery path and tested access to any encryption keys. \\
Theft, fire, or changing files from one compromised account & Approved copies separated by location or access; consider an offline or independently controlled copy. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

For personal documents you might have a working copy, a backup to
external storage, and an approved separate-location backup. An external
drive left plugged in can suffer the same accident as the computer,
and making encrypted backups is only useful if you can unlock them
when the original device is gone. Use the tools and retention
rules appropriate to your workplace or household; protecting data
must not mean moving it to a service you weren't authorized to use.
Agree on who can read the files, where encryption keys are stored
apart from the computer, and what to do when a person leaves the team.
An archive made with \cmd|tar| is not encrypted just because it is
a single file, and compressing it would not add privacy either.

Decide what is worth the space. A document can be irreplaceable
even if it takes only a few kilobytes, while a large downloaded
installer may be easy to obtain again. On the other hand, a
replacement installer doesn't recreate your settings or the
document you edited with it. For a project that spans several
applications, note which exports and source files you need to
recover together. Ask which accounts own material you only
appear to have because someone shared it with you. A shared
link isn't a backup. It can be revoked or point to the latest
version rather than the one you approved.

You needn't begin by choosing backup software. Before spending
money on storage, try writing a recovery sentence for one
folder: ``If February's minutes are damaged, I want the last
approved copy and can afford to wait until tomorrow to get
it back.'' That gives you a useful test. A daily backup might
be frequent enough, but it still needs a way to identify the
approved copy; a label in the document or a dated milestone
can be more reliable than guessing by modification time.

\section{Practice with two dated versions}
\index{tar@\texttt{tar}}\index{cksum@\texttt{cksum}}
This exercise uses two short fictional documents. All copies stay
under one temporary directory. The directories demonstrate roles;
they can't protect themselves against failure of the one filesystem
they live on. The syntax is for \texttt{sh}; stop if directory setup
fails. \cmd|umask 077| in the subshell below denies ordinary group and
other users permission bits on newly created objects. The parentheses
keep it from changing the outer shell's setting. It is not an
encryption guarantee or a replacement for checking destination access.

Each \texttt{\&\&} runs the next command only when the preceding
one succeeds. \texttt{mktemp} replaces the \texttt{X}s with a
unique suffix; \texttt{/tmp} here is the system's local temporary
directory. Use a different trusted absolute temporary-directory path
if your system requires one.

\begin{terminal}
backuplab=$(mktemp -d /tmp/utp-backup.XXXXXXXX) &&
  cd "$backuplab" &&
  (umask 077 && mkdir documents vault restore) &&
  pwd
\end{terminal}

Proceed only if the last line shows the new practice directory.
If it doesn't, do not run the rest of the lab. The following
redirections write new synthetic files; they would truncate existing
files of those names outside the fresh directory.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'Monday: one box of flyers' > documents/plan.txt &&
  printf '%s\n' 'Contact the community desk' > documents/contact.txt
\end{terminal}

This fixture uses ordinary files and a short path. For real
documents, make the copy when writers are idle or use the
application's documented consistent export or snapshot method.
A running database, a changing mail store, or a cloud placeholder
with no file bytes present is not equivalent to a closed text
document. A successful archive can represent a mix of dates if
a writer changed the source part way through. Likewise, files
behind symbolic links may not be copied when only the links are
archived. Inventory the files needed, including hidden files where
relevant, and test recovery of any required attributes or links.
Across filesystems and operating systems, owner IDs, access controls,
extended attributes, resource forks, and case distinctions may not
survive by default. Don't rely on this little text lab to prove they
will.

Make the first archive to a confirmed unused name outside the
document folder. Like tar creation in Chapter~\ref{ch:23},
\texttt{-c} means create and \texttt{-f} takes the archive pathname
that follows. \texttt{documents} is a relative input name,
including the directory itself and its contents. The archive file
is not in that directory, so the command does not try to archive
what it is writing. A date in a filename is a label, not magic:
make sure it identifies one particular run and use a distinct name
if you take multiple copies in one day. \cmd|tar -c| can overwrite
an existing archive. If there is already something at the destination,
stop rather than trying the example there.

\begin{terminal}
tar -cf vault/documents-2030-01-07.tar documents &&
  cksum < vault/documents-2030-01-07.tar > vault/jan07.cksum &&
  cat vault/jan07.cksum
\end{terminal}

The checksum runs only if tar succeeds. If the sequence fails, stop;
do not treat a partial archive or a partial checksum file as a backup.
\cmd|cksum| with no operand reads standard input; the
\texttt{<} supplies the archive bytes and \texttt{>} saves the
output to a separate new file. In the default POSIX form the output
is a checksum number and a byte count. Archive details such as stored
timestamps vary, so no particular numbers are expected. This kind
of checksum helps detect accidental corruption; it isn't designed
to prove authenticity against somebody who can change the archive
and its accompanying checksum. Keep an independent trusted record
when authenticity matters. Never upload private documents to a site
just to calculate a checksum.

There are two distinct checks you can run at different times.
Immediately after creating the backup, confirm you selected the
intended files and that anybody who will need to restore has a
legitimate way to access it. Later, check whether the bytes still
agree with what you recorded then. Calculating a new checksum and
discarding the previous one every time you check would remove the
very comparison you need. The practice writes the later reading
to another path and compares the two files. That also makes it
easier to diagnose a mistake if you have accidentally requested
the digest of the wrong archive.

Now deliberately change the working copy, not the archive, and
write a second archive. On real documents save changes in the
editor; here \texttt{>} replaces the entire content of the
fictional \texttt{plan.txt}. Check \cmd|pwd| first, and do not run
the redirection outside the lab.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'Tuesday: two boxes of flyers' > documents/plan.txt &&
  tar -cf vault/documents-2030-01-08.tar documents &&
  cksum < vault/documents-2030-01-08.tar > vault/jan08.cksum &&
  ls vault
\end{terminal}

Stop if the sequence fails. Expect
two named archives and two checksum files. The archives do not
replace one another, and both logical versions use the same
internal path, \texttt{documents/plan.txt}. A backup name should let
you determine when the copy was made and which source it covers.
A real backup catalog can also record where a copy lives, how to
access it, and when it was last restored. A catalog that names
sensitive clients or patients may itself require protection.
Notice the direction of the restore arrow in
Figure~\ref{fig:41-versions}: it doesn't point back to the working folder.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-41/versions.tikz}
\caption{Store dated archives apart from the working documents.}\label{fig:41-versions}
\VisualDescription{A working folder feeds two differently dated archives. The first goes to a separate restore folder, not back into the working folder.}
\end{figure}

\section{Restore into an empty directory and compare}\label{sec:41-restore}
\index{cmp@\texttt{cmp}}
Rehearse the loss now: get Monday's plan back while leaving Tuesday's
working version in place. Check the earlier archive against its saved
checksum before you extract. Use a new output path for the recomputed
record. \cmd|cmp| without options compares bytes and emits no ordinary
output on a match; immediately inspect the exit status. Zero is a match,
one is a difference, and a value greater than one is an error.

\begin{terminal}
cksum < vault/documents-2030-01-07.tar > ./restore/jan07.check
cmp ./restore/jan07.check ./vault/jan07.cksum
printf '%s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

An unexpected checksum is a reason to stop. Check that you compared
the intended version and that the checksum file was not changed.
Try a separate verified copy if the archive is damaged. Don't retry
the same extraction into the working documents. Notice that the
checksum record is a lab file in the restore area, not a file from
the archive. For an empty extraction destination create a dedicated
subdirectory under that area.

Before extraction, list the members of the trusted archive with
\texttt{-t} (list) and \texttt{-f} (archive path). Expect only
the directory and its two documents, though traversal order can
differ. A list of names doesn't establish that every file opens
or contains the expected revision. For unfamiliar archives, a list
alone is not a full security review: path traversal components,
absolute paths, links, and special files can have effects outside
the directory you intended. Practise only with the archive you made
from simple trusted files, without administrative privileges.

\begin{terminal}
tar -tf vault/documents-2030-01-07.tar
\end{terminal}

Stop if the listing fails or shows unexpected paths. If it checks out,
create a dedicated destination and inspect it. \texttt{-a} includes
names that begin with a dot: only \texttt{.} and \texttt{..} should
appear. If \texttt{mkdir} fails or there are other entries, stop.

\begin{terminal}
mkdir restore/monday
ls -a ./restore/monday
\end{terminal}

Extract only in the new empty directory. This
\texttt{-x} means extract; \texttt{-f} again supplies the filename.
Here a parenthesized subshell changes its own working directory;
\texttt{\&\&} runs tar only after a successful \texttt{cd}.
The \texttt{../../} part reaches the lab root from two levels
down. The surrounding shell's directory does not change.

\begin{terminal}
(cd ./restore/monday && tar -xf ../../vault/documents-2030-01-07.tar)
cat ./restore/monday/documents/plan.txt
cat ./documents/plan.txt
cmp ./restore/monday/documents/contact.txt ./documents/contact.txt
\end{terminal}

On success the two displayed plan lines should be Monday's
one box and Tuesday's two boxes, in that order. The contact
file was never changed, so comparing it should yield status zero.
That is a useful check on a known constant but says nothing about
whether the archived plan is the right snapshot; you inspected that
separately. With real documents, open representative files in their
normal application, check which revisions they are, and confirm that
any collaborator can use them as intended. Byte checks, human checks,
and application checks serve different purposes.
Figure~\ref{fig:41-drill} includes verification twice: once for
the bundle and again for the separate files you recovered.

If you actually need an older revision, stop after restoring it
and compare it with the live one. Your aim may be to recover a
single table or note, not roll back a whole folder with other
people's current work in it. A restored version can also contain
old mistakes or access settings you shouldn't reintroduce. Keep
the verified recovered version apart until you have decided what
will be merged back, make a separate copy of the current version
before replacing anything, and have collaborators review any
proposed rollback. A successful restore demonstrates that you have
a choice, not that you should automatically choose the older copy.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-41/drill.tikz}
\caption{A restore drill checks both the archive and its recovered files.}\label{fig:41-drill}
\VisualDescription{Choose a date, verify and list the archive, extract into a clean location, and open or compare the recovered files.}
\end{figure}

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.45in}Y}
\toprule
Observation & What you can reasonably claim \\
\midrule
The backup job reported success & It thought it made a copy; check scope, location, and status. \\
Saved checksum matches & The current archive agrees with the recorded checksum for this version, subject to the checksum's limits. \\
The expected files open after extraction & This version is recoverable in this test environment using the access you had for the drill. \\
A second device can restore independently & The tested copy is not dependent on access to the first device. Test credentials and keys as part of that drill. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{dialoguebox}{Which copy is safe?}
\Brian{I have five copies of the newsletter. Surely one will save me.}
\Stephen{Five is a good start. Where are they?}
\Brian{Five dated folders on the same drive.}
\Stephen{Then they help with an accidental edit, but not a failed drive.}
\Brian{What if I move them all to the same cloud account?}
\Stephen{That changes the failure you're protected from. Now ask what happens if you lose the account.}
\Brian{So count the failures these copies can survive, not the folders.}
\Stephen{And prove it with a restore drill.}
\DialogueConclusion{Separate copies by the failures they must survive.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Write down what the next person must do}
Keep a short recovery note with the backups: when the job runs,
which folders are included, where the copies live, who has
approved access, and the steps to restore a dated version to a
new location. Do not include a passphrase in the same note as an
encrypted backup unless that is part of a securely managed recovery
procedure. Repeat the drill after replacing drives, moving accounts,
or changing the software. Record the actual restore time once,
including any wait for a key or network transfer. If restoring a
large project would take two days, a note saying ``quick restore''
is misleading even if you genuinely have the data.

A useful recovery note can be followed when you are not the
person who wrote it. Give the date format, explain which copy
should be the first to try, and point to the approved method of
gaining access when the ordinary device is gone. If your only
instructions are on the disk you are protecting, test whether a
spare copy of the instructions is available too. If you manage
backups for a group, make sure more than one authorized person
knows who has the key and where the policy is documented. A
one-time restore by the account owner isn't a test that someone
else can do the job.

Retention is a decision, not an instruction to delete whichever
archive is largest. A policy might keep daily versions for a week
and monthly versions for a year, but organizational or legal rules
can require different time limits. Confirm recovery options before
retiring any copy; inspect what would remain before removing
anything. For storage usage, see Chapter~\ref{ch:34}. For a second
location, Chapter~\ref{ch:39} shows how to verify an authorized
transfer. No network copying is needed in this chapter.

\begin{exercise}{Identify the older snapshot}\label{ex:41-identify}
List the members of the older archive and confirm that they include
the plan and the contact note. Does that list alone tell you which day
the restored plan will mention? This creates no additional files.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\cmd|tar -tf vault/documents-2030-01-07.tar| lists
\texttt{documents/plan.txt} and \texttt{documents/contact.txt},
and the directory entry. The name of the plan is the same both days.
The restore-and-read test distinguishes them.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Restore the second day without overwriting the first}\label{ex:41-second}
Repeat the verification and extraction for January 8. Recompute its
checksum to a new path \texttt{restore/jan08.check}, then use a new
directory \texttt{restore/tuesday}. Compare the two restored plan files
while keeping the working files. Save both restored copies for cleanup.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\cmd|cksum < vault/documents-2030-01-08.tar > restore/jan08.check|
records the check; \cmd|cmp restore/jan08.check vault/jan08.cksum|
should match. Check the member list as before; then
\cmd|mkdir restore/tuesday| and
\cmd|(cd restore/tuesday && tar -xf ../../vault/documents-2030-01-08.tar)|.
Comparing the plan paths under \texttt{restore/monday} and
\texttt{restore/tuesday} should report a difference and return status
one. Check \cmd|cat| to see each day's text.
\end{solution}

\paragraph{Lab cleanup.}
After the exercises, confirm that \cmd|pwd| agrees with
\cmd|"$backuplab"| before removing anything. Confirm only the
named synthetic files at the \cmd|rm -i| prompts. If you didn't
do the Tuesday restore exercise, skip the lines that name items
under \texttt{restore/tuesday} and skip \texttt{restore/jan08.check};
still remove both dated archives and both saved vault checksums.
If a practice step failed part way through, inspect what it did
create before removing those entries. Stop on anything unexpected.
\begin{terminal}
rm -i documents/plan.txt documents/contact.txt
rm -i restore/monday/documents/plan.txt restore/monday/documents/contact.txt
rm -i restore/tuesday/documents/plan.txt restore/tuesday/documents/contact.txt
rm -i restore/jan07.check
rm -i restore/jan08.check
rm -i vault/documents-2030-01-07.tar vault/documents-2030-01-08.tar
rm -i vault/jan07.cksum vault/jan08.cksum
rmdir restore/monday/documents restore/monday
rmdir restore/tuesday/documents restore/tuesday
rmdir documents restore vault
cd && rmdir "$backuplab"
\end{terminal}
The final \cmd|rmdir| should succeed only after all the expected
practice entries have been removed.

\begin{exercise}{Retire the practice data, not your documents}\label{ex:41-cleanup}
Write down a recovery check you'd add for your real documents.
Then verify \cmd|pwd| agrees with \cmd|"$backuplab"| and remove
the synthetic lab with the cleanup commands above.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
An example is opening one restored budget sheet in its normal editor,
checking its formula results and date. Keep this check separate from
the cleanup of synthetic files in the lab.
\end{solution}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Plan around a specific loss; agreement across devices isn't a history.
\item Retain distinct dated versions and keep copies separate from the systems they protect.
\item Restore to a new location and verify the usable content before trusting the plan.
\end{takeaways}

